Jobs are service-private background execution. They are useful for work that should continue independently of a caller-facing request and may need retry, progress tracking, or dead-letter handling.

The Trellis jobs runtime provides a service-private job queue with retry, progress tracking, and dead-letter handling. Jobs use a stream-first architecture: JetStream is the source of truth, and the Jobs admin runtime keeps a SQL projection for queries.

Job states include `pending`, `active`, `retry`, `completed`, `failed`,
`cancelled`, `expired`, `skipped`, `stale`, `dead`, and `dismissed`.

Keyed queues can skip queued work by policy. Lease expiration alone does not
settle a job. Ordinary queues use Block: the same job can recover its expired
slot, while other jobs wait. At the coordinator's fail-stale boundary, takeover
fences the old attempt but preserves its cleanup obligation; only its recovered
owner can publish Stale after reconciliation under a fresh fence. This policy is
not an authored queue option. Dead-lettered jobs can be replayed or dismissed by
an operator.

A keyed worker acquires the key slot before starting handler work. While capacity
is unavailable, it maintains the broker delivery lease with progress
acknowledgements; this wait does not consume retry attempts or trigger
max-delivery exhaustion. Retryable failures after the handler starts do consume
the job's retry policy.

Broker redelivery is unlimited, independently of the ordinary execution-attempt
budget. Previously started jobs reach Expired or Dead only after their execution
owner reconciles earlier side effects with cancellation already requested.
Cleanup errors preserve redelivery. Never-started expired jobs release any
recovered key slot without running the handler. The janitor does not settle
deadlines or perform application cleanup.

Services process their own jobs via their own JetStream consumer. A central Jobs admin runtime handles global queries, janitor cleanup, and the SQL projection for the built-in Jobs API.

`Jobs.Query` returns cursor pages. `Jobs.Summary` applies the same filters to all
matching projected jobs and returns page-independent totals, state statistics,
and optional groups.

Jobs are not visible to callers. Caller-observable async work should use an operation instead.
